ref="/metric-admission/"><span>19</span>Source metrics</a><a href="/subdivision/"><span>20</span>Subdivision</a><a href="/metric-selection/"><span>21</span>Metric selection</a><a href="/refinement-tower/"><span>22</span>Refinement tower</a><a href="/history-comparison/"><span>23</span>History comparison</a></nav><div class="sidebar-bottom"><span class="status-dot"></span> Physical promotion <b>0</b><p>Independent human review<br>pending</p><a href="https://github.com/ud-tetra/UD">Research repository ↗</a></div></aside><div class="page-shell"><header class="topbar"><span>FINITE STRUCTURE / OPEN CORRESPONDENCE</span><a href="/sources/">Search the record <span>⌕</span></a></header><main id="main"><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">Evolution and its limits</div><h1>What the equations move—and what they do not select.</h1><p class="lede">A fixed-complex flow is explicit. Native event selection and cross-event interfaces are additional dynamical laws.</p></div><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>The fixed-carrier baseline.</h2><p>Declare K, signed B, unit metric, initial chain c(0) and the closed generator A. Then c(s) = exp(sA)c(0) is determined and its total support is conserved. The generator acts within one chain space; it does not choose a new complex, trigger a geometry event, or prescribe its state transfer.</p><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=PI">PI · §§4, 7.4 ↗</a></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>A hard gate is a predicate, not an event clock.</h2><div class="equation">hₑ = Cₑ Tₑ &nbsp; · &nbsp; Cₑ,Tₑ ∈ {0,1}</div><p>Closure and transport-integrity factors determine whether a supplied gate is open. Observing their product generally cannot identify which factor closed it. Under dynamics that depend only on the product, even all pre-event amplitude derivatives can leave the factor states indistinguishable.</p><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R09">R09 · factor-state audit §§1–3 ↗</a><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=A20">A20 · Q156–Q173 ↗</a><aside class="callout"><strong>OPEN — source-selected transition</strong><p>A predictive event needs typed pre-event inputs, an address, factor-update law, timing, admissibility and a tie rule. Counterfactual probes and hand-chosen thresholds remain candidate additions.</p></aside></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">03</div><div><h2>Event counts and time.</h2><p>Scheduling by edge reversal on a bipartite vertex–cell incidence graph is a discrete counting programme. Relative heights and component offsets describe scheduling information. They do not supply a spacetime metric or a clock in seconds.</p><p>Likewise, an exact phase period in a matrix flow does not calibrate event duration. The current source catalog includes event-time manuscripts and LC bench/estimator records.</p><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=M01">M01 · items 5 and 7 ↗</a> <a class="text-link" href="/sources/?q=event%20time">Find event-time records →</a></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">04</div><div><h2>Shape agreement, retention and growth.</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Required observation</th><th>Current boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coherent agreement</td><td>Declared compatibility at a snapshot</td><td>Does not imply persistence</td></tr><tr><td>Persistence</td><td>Retention over a specified interval and perturbation set</td><td>Prepared retention is conditional</td></tr><tr><td>Growth preference</td><td>Competing motifs under equal resources and admission rules</td><td>Native selection remains open</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Fresh-face attachment proves stacked-ball counts; both chain and star patterns remain possible. A nonregular icosahedral cone is a supplied construction, not evidence that regular tetrahedra universally select that shape.</p><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=PIII">PIII · §§11–12 ↗</a></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">05</div><div><h2>Refinement has an interface cost.</h2><p>The October 13 bounded-subdivision candidate transfers part of native support into a residual carrier. Its two-event support ledger is conditional. It does not preserve unit vertex readout or intertwine the unweighted native generators.</p><div class="equation">native support: 1 → 5/8 → 25/64 &nbsp; · &nbsp; total with residuals: 1</div><p>The strict interface contract—contraction, unit singleton vertex coefficient, and native-generator intertwining—fails on K₄ → sd K₄ because the incident degree changes from 3 to 7. Changing the readout, weights or event defect creates a new branch.</p><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R33">R33 · bounded transfer §§2–4 ↗</a> <a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R34">R34 · interface gate §2 ↗</a></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">06</div><div><h2>Record entropy and retained information.</h2><div id="information"></div><p>Record entropy depends on declared coarse-graining. Snapshot entropy need not increase; accumulated trajectory information is a different observable. Boundary-memory scaling requires an actual memory carrier and a full account of writing, storage, faults and renewal.</p><p>The October 13 receipt apparatus has a 34-slot decomposed write. A 44-slot deadline leaves 10 storage rounds; 32 rounds after writing require 66 total slots. The thermal-gap frontier is a requirement on candidate apparatus, not a native derivation of its gaps or temperature.</p><a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R36">R36 · session summary ↗</a> <a class="citation" href="/sources/?id=R40">R40 · clock and non-identifiability ↗</a><aside class="callout"><strong>OPEN — full resource cost</strong><p>Bath swaps export correlations. Controller, reservoir restoration, physical time, energy and area scales must be supplied before a complete efficiency or horizon-area correspondence is claimed.</p></aside></div></section></main><footer><div><a href="/">Unified Dynamics</a><p>Benjamin Walker Mayes · Independent researcher<br>AI-assisted research · Public reading edition 0.14</p></div><div><a href="/sources/">Sources & coverage</a><a href="/contribution/">Contribution</a><a href="https://tetrahedral-packet-complexes.ben-w-mayes.chatgpt.site/">Living research portal ↗</a></div><p class="footer-rule">Proof, simulation and physical correspondence retain distinct evidence status. Physical promotion: 0.</p></footer></div></body></html>
