+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Unified Dynamics public research atlas: finite tetrahedral mathematics, declared dynamics, source provenance and independently testable physical questions."><meta name="theme-color" content="#f7f6ef"><title>Source metric admission · Unified Dynamics</title><link rel="icon" href="/assets/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/style.css"><script src="/assets/app.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/metric-admission-v0.1.js" defer></script></head><body data-page="metric-admission"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><aside class="sidebar"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 3L3 33H37Z M20 3L22 23L3 33 M22 23L37 33"/></svg><div>Unified<br>Dynamics<small>PUBLIC RESEARCH ATLAS</small></div></a><button class="menu-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="navigation">Explore <span>☰</span></button><nav id="navigation" aria-label="Primary"><a href="/" ><span>01</span>Overview</a><a href="/primer/" ><span>02</span>Primer</a><a href="/mathematics/" ><span>03</span>Mathematics</a><a href="/dynamics/" ><span>04</span>Dynamics</a><a href="/evidence/" ><span>05</span>Evidence</a><a href="/papers/" ><span>06</span>Papers</a><a href="/sources/" ><span>07</span>Sources</a><a href="/history/" ><span>08</span>History</a><a href="/contribution/" ><span>09</span>Contribution</a><a href="/updates/" ><span>10</span>Updates</a><a href="/reconciliation/"><span>11</span>Reconciliation</a><a href="/dependencies/"><span>12</span>Dependencies</a><a href="/source-contract/"><span>13</span>Source contract</a><a href="/source-programme/"><span>14</span>Source programme</a><a href="/realization/"><span>15</span>Realization</a><a href="/joint-carrier/"><span>16</span>Joint carrier</a><a href="/programmed-clock/"><span>17</span>Programmed clock</a><a href="/native-admission/"><span>18</span>Native admission</a><a href="/metric-admission/" aria-current="page"><span>19</span>Source metrics</a><a href="/subdivision/"><span>20</span>Subdivision</a><a href="/metric-selection/"><span>21</span>Metric selection</a><a href="/refinement-tower/"><span>22</span>Refinement tower</a><a href="/history-comparison/"><span>23</span>History comparison</a><a href="/observation/"><span>24</span>Observation</a></nav><div class="sidebar-bottom"><span class="status-dot"></span> Physical promotion <b>0</b><p>Independent human review<br>pending</p><a href="https://github.com/ud-tetra/UD">Research repository ↗</a></div></aside><div class="page-shell"><header class="topbar"><span>FINITE STRUCTURE / OPEN CORRESPONDENCE</span><a href="/sources/">Search the record <span>⌕</span></a></header><main id="main"><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">Source metric admission v0.1 · development</div><h1>Changing the metric needs a new test.</h1><p class="lede">The sources contain more than the unit-metric carrier. Correctly normalizing their operators reveals three distinct obstructions to the full default clock, including a geometric family with five bands.</p></div>
 <aside class="callout"><strong>Scoped exact results · physical promotion 0</strong><p>These tests allow a common rate and arbitrarily many identical copies, with the same metric and coefficients in every copy. They concern complete generator equality. Native parameter selection and independent human review remain open.</p></aside>
 <section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>Keep the two metric conventions separate</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Declared evolution</th><th>Normalized degree coupling</th><th>Scale every metric by s</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fixed boundary B, with its G-adjoint</td><td>√(g(k−1)/gk)</td><td>Rates unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Mass equation G ċ=A0c</td><td>1/√(g(k−1)gk)</td><td>Rates divided by s</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The historical inverse-product formula belongs to the mass equation. C0496 already repairs that distinction. Both use a conserved G pairing; neither chooses a physical norm or energy.</p><a class="text-link" href="/sources/?id=C0496">Read the source correction →</a></div></section>
 <section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>Which full-generator routes are excluded?</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Source family</th><th>Obstruction</th><th>Clock scope</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Real S4-equivariant skew generator, any positive invariant metric</td><td>At most three positive bands</td><td>Even n≥8</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed geometry, Gram Rule A or B</td><td>Five bands obey u/v+x/y=2; all 120 default assignments fail</td><td>Default n=10; band count for n≥12</td></tr><tr><td>Degree-weighted K− or K+</td><td>Irrational paired ratios allow at most three clock-grid matches</td><td>Even n≥8</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Copies expand multiplicities and preserve the frequency set. Heterogeneous or coupled copies, a different complex, and a broader declared operator need fresh premises. The geometric result leaves n=8 unresolved.</p><p>The three-band default comparison has an exact relaxed residual floor η/κ=1, attained by free centers 3,7,10. These are mathematical witnesses, not adopted couplings. A residual floor does not bound actual endpoint error from below.</p></div></section>
