+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Unified Dynamics public research atlas: finite tetrahedral mathematics, declared dynamics, source provenance and independently testable physical questions."><meta name="theme-color" content="#f7f6ef"><title>Metric selection · Unified Dynamics</title><link rel="icon" href="/assets/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/style.css"><script src="/assets/app.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/metric-selection-v0.1.js" defer></script></head><body data-page="metric-selection"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><aside class="sidebar"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 3L3 33H37Z M20 3L22 23L3 33 M22 23L37 33"/></svg><div>Unified<br>Dynamics<small>PUBLIC RESEARCH ATLAS</small></div></a><button class="menu-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="navigation">Explore <span>☰</span></button><nav id="navigation" aria-label="Primary"><a href="/" ><span>01</span>Overview</a><a href="/primer/" ><span>02</span>Primer</a><a href="/mathematics/" ><span>03</span>Mathematics</a><a href="/dynamics/" ><span>04</span>Dynamics</a><a href="/evidence/" ><span>05</span>Evidence</a><a href="/papers/" ><span>06</span>Papers</a><a href="/sources/" ><span>07</span>Sources</a><a href="/history/" ><span>08</span>History</a><a href="/contribution/" ><span>09</span>Contribution</a><a href="/updates/" ><span>10</span>Updates</a><a href="/reconciliation/"><span>11</span>Reconciliation</a><a href="/dependencies/"><span>12</span>Dependencies</a><a href="/source-contract/"><span>13</span>Source contract</a><a href="/source-programme/"><span>14</span>Source programme</a><a href="/realization/"><span>15</span>Realization</a><a href="/joint-carrier/"><span>16</span>Joint carrier</a><a href="/programmed-clock/"><span>17</span>Programmed clock</a><a href="/native-admission/"><span>18</span>Native admission</a><a href="/metric-admission/"><span>19</span>Source metrics</a><a href="/subdivision/"><span>20</span>Subdivision</a><a href="/metric-selection/" aria-current="page"><span>21</span>Metric selection</a><a href="/refinement-tower/"><span>22</span>Refinement tower</a><a href="/history-comparison/"><span>23</span>History comparison</a><a href="/observation/"><span>24</span>Observation</a></nav><div class="sidebar-bottom"><span class="status-dot"></span> Physical promotion <b>0</b><p>Independent human review<br>pending</p><a href="https://github.com/ud-tetra/UD">Research repository ↗</a></div></aside><div class="page-shell"><header class="topbar"><span>FINITE STRUCTURE / OPEN CORRESPONDENCE</span><a href="/sources/">Search the record <span>⌕</span></a></header><main id="main"><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">Metric and observable selection v0.1 · development</div><h1>Selection needs a source commitment.</h1><p class="lede">The native unit metric and simplex stocks are fixed on each carrier. Across subdivision, compatibility and symmetry leave metric and observer freedom. An explicit conditional rule can select both; its additional premises remain visible.</p></div><aside class="callout"><strong>Exact conditional selector · native adoption open</strong><p>No current governed source selects the added affine readout, unit residual calibration or complement-blind observer. Their algebraic consequences are proved below. Constructor-team verification; human review pending; physical promotion0.</p></aside>
 <section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>What is fixed, and what is still a choice?</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Commitment</th><th>Exact consequence</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Native stock Σcσ² in the declared basis</td><td>Metric I and region projector D_U</td><td>Existing fixed-carrier source</td></tr><tr><td>Norm, boundary and full generator compatibility</td><td>Chain retraction plus positive complement pairing</td><td>Exact classification; no unique metric</td></tr><tr><td>Positive observable calibrated only on the old code</td><td>Complement response remains free</td><td>Exact nonselection witness</td></tr><tr><td>Affine readout, calibrated orthogonal residual, blind observer</td><td>Unique R, H* and K_U</td><td>Additional candidate premises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>An arbitrary positive diagonal fine metric cannot repair ordinary subdivision's full generator equation. Its adjoint cannot create a flag coefficient that the transported old generator requires. This preserves the original local-stock obstruction.</p></div></section>
 <section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>Inspect a concrete nonselection witness</h2><p>The same fine state is the unit vertex at barycenter {0,1}. Its decoded coarse norm is1/2 and residual Euclidean norm is3/2. Every positive metric H_w=RᵀR+wQᵀQ passes the three compatibility and symmetry tests.</p><div class="search-tools"><label>Residual metric weight w<select id="ms-weight"><option value="0.5">1/2</option><option value="1" selected>1</option><option value="2">2</option></select></label><label>Observer complement response λ<select id="ms-response"><option value="0" selected>0 · complement-blind</option><option value="0.5">1/2</option><option value="1">1 · full response</option></select></label></div><p id="ms-verdict" role="status"></p><div id="ms-witness"></div><p id="ms-receipts"></p><p>These controls are mathematical counterexamples. They adopt no metric coefficient. The readout numerator is RᵀR+λwQᵀQ; changing it can leave old-code calibration unchanged while changing the fine-state result.</p></div></section>
 <section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">03</div><div><h2>The conditional rule that selects both</h2><p>First require face-local affine readout, normalized and covariant under relabeling, with exterior consistency in higher degrees. That fixes the barycentric chain retraction R. Let Q=I−SR.</p><div class="callout"><p class="form">H*=RᵀGR+QᵀQ</p><p>This follows uniquely if the old sector and retained complement are orthogonal, the coarse norm is calibrated to G, and the complement norm is explicitly calibrated to its unit Euclidean value.</p><p class="form">K_U=RᵀD_U R</p><p>This follows uniquely for a positive coarse observable calibrated on Sc and blind to kerR. Positivity removes hidden cross terms.</p></div><p>These are declared mathematical premises. They do not follow merely from symmetry or the absence of adjustable parameters. A constitutive or measurement source must justify adoption.</p></div></section>
